#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459327} :activity/id #uuid "eb4fa799-1cf3-5f04-b3e8-6491d1930019" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130 :topic/id #uuid "81ceba80-b7bc-5614-b160-458b9437b8ca" :topic/math-academy-id 881} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99d53a9-e3cd-5940-92bd-1ac167fee043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{4x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371093} {:answer/id #uuid "0194c198-f1ba-5188-a3b2-8be3db8cc0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3{e}^{-2x}-{e}^{4x}+3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371094} {:answer/id #uuid "e9322655-e73f-5cbe-9415-3c468c5fccf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371095} {:answer/id #uuid "d28e37d2-b4e2-571c-994c-728cba570d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-3x}+3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371096} {:answer/id #uuid "0219f8c0-8746-5f02-a66a-b7d3eee1308d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+3{e}^{4x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371093} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca78cc66-c01e-58dd-9a61-3727fb6f5bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7eb42f98-90c1-5eda-85e2-0c63eff353a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-7900" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=16x-4,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha -8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=16x-4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha x-(2\\alpha +8\\beta )=16x-4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha =16\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+4B{e}^{4x}-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A+4B=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -2A+4B=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-1,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{4x}-2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0db1c32e-a0d9-5c5f-8774-5819bd811d6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccfb431-8d63-5996-b9dd-56c7e2c91897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355349} {:answer/id #uuid "672cae62-2b06-5798-accf-018bb4098775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355579} {:answer/id #uuid "7351ba0a-f089-5b15-b307-93d1dafbe5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355680} {:answer/id #uuid "a207c9a6-bd25-52b5-a6bd-0bf7d6fcfcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356106} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b88408-feae-5731-87a6-b3bb0bdd717f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356165} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc8e124-28b9-5e53-895c-b00b093ac14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0975d216-0aaa-5035-b1c0-1872bde3d271" :question/math-academy-id "q-94258" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A+B & =0 \\\\ -4(1)+B & =0 \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4749f8ab-c642-5010-b2b1-b53de10d2f10"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000ff1ff-0514-51c0-9f4a-bb1ea4d39d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458614} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd8de66-80f8-585f-bb48-c7c78917024a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458647} {:answer/id #uuid "20c0be51-5d2f-5c05-9739-98e7ea336cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{5x}+2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458690} {:answer/id #uuid "5e53e89b-d317-52dc-98dc-3ac1d653c4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458787} {:answer/id #uuid "aa182a9a-ec77-55c7-ba18-711fbbda1c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186458933} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8b734c-02f2-5ab4-af4d-770fc2d60b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cebb3523-8077-5ac7-812f-20bb6712f59f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7899" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=-10{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =5$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-10{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha {x}^{2}-(6\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta -10\\gamma )=-10{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-10\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 6\\alpha +10\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta -10\\gamma =-\\frac{1}{2}\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7da25dee-65b9-585e-a311-49b0e98a5770" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ea37fc-b3b6-5e0f-b118-0785b2b7a35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371114} {:answer/id #uuid "e49653a9-dd2d-51bf-bd9d-5842cb840b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371115} {:answer/id #uuid "ba043dd9-87a7-5698-8627-c0ea7930279c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371116} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ee0dc8-6c03-5f38-91c3-4560dc41ea14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371117} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe728d0-4e52-55b2-a24a-c60915ca9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371118}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371115} :answer-field/id #uuid "f595e04a-614b-5d7a-9cbf-9e5ecfb07f11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21fd8f0b-3109-5022-9a47-313751ce31ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-8033" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+13y=39x+1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+13y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+13 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2\\pm 3\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +13(\\alpha x+\\beta )=39x+1\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n13\\alpha x+(13\\beta -4\\alpha )=39x+1.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}13\\alpha =39\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 13\\beta -4\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d973ae11-a0e0-5ab1-9997-b3e7e835583a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4b1bd9-a788-5338-b6a3-0321efca35f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+3x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403107} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3a8c12-b522-5cd4-80bc-d4e5e857d33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403108} {:answer/id #uuid "56190e71-ba59-515b-a03d-ccc2b7e5bdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}-x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403109} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1d2035-a1c4-5242-b59f-70997c2bf914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403110} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb4f8b5-aafd-5f68-a0c9-e62da167d11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403111} :answer-field/id #uuid "62409f98-64c5-5617-a551-69fb536c4027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43efe1d0-c638-55a1-8bd9-f43f26e2bc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-64206" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d98fbe2b-4a5f-51a4-a81f-b73bffb88daa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459323}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Polynomial Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458613}]}
